{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"kneelength-flawless-lobo","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Context / Problem\nT11's POST-ROLLBACK SCHEMA STATE paragraph is a stock-finding generator: 8 of 21 sampled findings were near-identical \"plan does not assert post-rollback state\" advisories that are tautologies against an append-only store (median priority ~0.1), drowning its rare real catches (e.g. the 114f fold-in overwrite data-loss bug). The operator approved a constraint and rejected a per-plan cap; the constraint must be portable across stacks (no store technology named).\n\n## Approach\nReplace the post-rollback paragraph in src/rebar/llm/reviewers/plan_review_T11.md with a four-condition applicability rule: raise post-rollback-state findings ONLY when the plan (a) changes how existing persisted records are interpreted, (b) rewrites or migrates records in place, (c) removes or narrows a read path, or (d) performs a one-way transformation with no back-out. Add the explicit anti-false-positive line: purely additive, backward-compatible persistence changes that existing readers ignore (new record/event types, optional fields, new tables or columns with defaults) do not qualify — residual data after rollback is the norm in append-only and backward-compatible systems, not a risk. No store technology is named, so the rule applies equally to SQL migrations, event stores, document databases, and config formats. Posture stays advisory. Alternative rejected: a one-finding-per-plan cap — operator rejected it; the applicability rule removes the tautology class at the source instead.\n\n## Scope\nsrc/rebar/llm/reviewers/plan_review_T11.md, tests/llm/test_t11_rubric.py, docs/plan-review-criteria-guide.md (regenerated).\n\n## Testing\nRubric text assertions: the four conditions and the anti-FP line are present; the old unconditional post-rollback wording is gone; no store-technology token (SQL, event store, append-only-as-requirement) appears in the applicability rule itself beyond the illustrative anti-FP examples.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] The rubric raises post-rollback findings only under the four named conditions (checked: rubric text unit test asserting the conditions and absence of the old wording).\n- [ ] The anti-FP line for additive backward-compatible changes is present (checked: rubric text test).\n- [ ] The criteria guide is regenerated (checked: committed regen output).","id":"ef01-79fe-f287-4fec","parent_id":"a492-3e46-ca44-40a5","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"task","title":"Portable persistence-safety constraint for the T11 migration rubric"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1785384394621335001,"uuid":"75155890-8746-4f7e-bbec-316270faa18b"}